diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..38af8176012e59038fbca22027643d1f308232c1
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,192 @@
+# arXiv 2601.04295 — verified pair-in-a-block witnesses
+
+**Status:** the generator and every local gate pass. The mandatory four-vendor
+oracle claim is **not yet established**: OpenRouter rejected all bare and G9 calls
+with HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded`. The script-owned error transcripts are retained,
+and the run must be repeated after quota is restored.
+
+| Profile field | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B** — no-tool compression |
+| Native domain | combinatorics |
+| Object regime | finite discrete |
+| Computational core | other (exact block-incidence witness search) |
+| Certificate form | integer tuple, represented as a selected pair plus its six-element block |
+| Intended intuition | decomposition: recognize repeated 60-point cells and the paired base-block pattern |
+| Domain essentiality | native |
+| Reduction | none |
+
+## What the family asks
+
+[Gomes, “An explicit family of 30 blocks meeting every 6-set of [60] in at
+least two points”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.04295) constructs 30 six-element
+blocks and proves in Theorem 1 that every selected 6-set contains a pair lying in
+one block. An instance here hands the solver a selected set and a shuffled family
+of six-blocks. The answer is the paper's native witness: two selected points and
+one listed block containing both. `verify` checks types, ordering, selected-set
+membership, block-family membership, and the two containments exactly.
+
+Generation never solves its output. It takes `n` disjoint copies of Section 2.
+Exactly one copy receives six selected points in Theorem 1's second-case pattern;
+each other copy receives five points, one from one base block in each paired
+couple. Consequently there is exactly one certified pair–block witness. Added
+decoy blocks contain exactly one selected point, so they cannot introduce another
+witness. This is composition of the paper's identity followed by a
+certificate-preserving augmentation.
+
+## Why this is Track B
+
+The paper proves correctness, not hardness. Its single 60-point example is easy:
+Section 3's proof classifies six points into ten base blocks and five paired
+couples in a handful of operations. Track A would therefore be false.
+
+For the composed, shuffled instances, the domain-standard algorithm is a direct
+incidence scan. It is exact, runs in `O(6m)` for `m` listed blocks, and succeeds
+8/8 as expected. At the current shipping preset it averaged **4,288 membership
+tests and 0.00348 seconds**. That is easy for software but not a plausible manual
+route through a 43,930-character prompt. The compact route notices that sorted
+selected points have an almost constant histogram across consecutive 60-point
+cells, locates the exceptional cell by binary search, and applies Theorem 1's
+paired-base-block rule there. The measured maximum was 61 exact operations; the
+conservative bound is **73**. The gap, rather than an unclaimed complexity lower
+bound, is the Track B hardness basis.
+
+## Worked demo (`n=1`, seed `0`)
+
+This is the complete rendered demo and is hand-solvable.
+
+```text
+PAIR-IN-A-BLOCK WITNESS
+
+The ground set is the integers 1 through 60, inclusive.
+A block is an unordered set of exactly six distinct ground-set integers.
+The selected set S is:
+3 22 34 39 47 53
+
+The block family has 30 blocks. Block numbering below is only for readability:
+0: 4 5 6 10 11 12
+1: 1 2 3 10 11 12
+2: 16 17 18 19 20 21
+3: 1 2 3 4 5 6
+4: 37 38 39 43 44 45
+5: 28 29 30 31 32 33
+6: 13 14 15 19 20 21
+7: 43 44 45 46 47 48
+8: 25 26 27 31 32 33
+9: 28 29 30 34 35 36
+10: 4 5 6 7 8 9
+11: 37 38 39 40 41 42
+12: 31 32 33 34 35 36
+13: 7 8 9 10 11 12
+14: 37 38 39 46 47 48
+15: 13 14 15 22 23 24
+16: 1 2 3 7 8 9
+17: 13 14 15 16 17 18
+18: 40 41 42 46 47 48
+19: 52 53 54 55 56 57
+20: 49 50 51 58 59 60
+21: 49 50 51 55 56 57
+22: 16 17 18 22 23 24
+23: 49 50 51 52 53 54
+24: 25 26 27 34 35 36
+25: 19 20 21 22 23 24
+26: 40 41 42 43 44 45
+27: 52 53 54 58 59 60
+28: 55 56 57 58 59 60
+29: 25 26 27 28 29 30
+
+Find two distinct elements x<y of S and one listed block containing both x and y.
+Return the block itself, not its displayed line number. Write both arrays in strictly increasing order.
+Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags as one JSON object of the exact form
+<answer>{"pair":[x,y],"block":[b1,b2,b3,b4,b5,b6]}</answer>
+where all placeholders are decimal integers. Output nothing else inside the tags.
+```
+
+The answer is
+`{"pair":[39,47],"block":[37,38,39,46,47,48]}` and `verify` returns
+`(True, "ok")`. Dropping `47` from the pair returns
+`(False, "pair must contain exactly two integers")`.
+
+## Difficulty ladder
+
+| Preset | `n` copies | Decoys/copy | Selected points | Blocks | Candidate space |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| demo | 1 | 0 | 6 | 30 | 450 |
+| easy (**current shipping**) | 32 | 12 | 161 | 1,344 | 17,310,720 |
+| medium | 40 | 24 | 201 | 2,160 | 43,416,000 |
+| hard | 50 | 36 | 251 | 3,300 | 103,537,500 |
+
+No preset has been rejected by a correctness or adversary gate. The final
+shipping label remains provisional until the bare hardening loop receives three
+non-error vendor responses; if `easy` is solved, `harden.py` owns escalation.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| Gate | Result | Measurement |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | 12/12 planted witnesses; independently counted exactly one answer each |
+| G2 | pass | 5/5 corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass | tagged JSON recovered through surrounding prose and a Markdown fence |
+| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 hits; exact density `1/17,310,720 = 5.7768e-8` |
+| G5 | pass | exact shipping count 1; strongest failing attack used 2,048 restart trials total |
+| G6 | pass | four attacks at 0/8; direct scan and compact reference each solve 8/8 |
+| G7 | pass | doubling `n` gives 2,688 blocks and a 138,055,680-candidate space; witness verifies |
+| G8 | pass | 60/60 invariance and carried-witness checks; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | pass | 52 characters, 13 estimated tokens, 8 atoms, 73 intended operations |
+
+The four failing attacks are selected-point degree outliers, the closest-value
+greedy pair, 256 structure-aware random restarts, and the paper's first-case
+same-base-block ansatz. Balanced selected-point decoy incidences remove the first
+signal; uniform block shuffling removes position; construction forbids the
+same-base case; the unique witness makes restarts sparse.
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 arms
+
+The bare run reached no scoreable oracle attempt. These rows are script-authored,
+not manually synthesized.
+
+| Arm | Preset | Seeds attempted | Valid solved/attempts | Outcome |
+|---|---|---|---:|---|
+| bare | easy | 306681586, 782815906, 853976121, 1716438569 | 0/0 | 4 HTTP 403 key-limit errors; no verdict |
+| structural | easy | 425005883, 756326095, 2136530550, 1739707 | 0/0 | 4 HTTP 403 key-limit errors; no verdict |
+| placebo | easy | 108855137, 1183835539, 1460789288, 1943948061 | 0/0 | 4 HTTP 403 key-limit errors; no verdict |
+
+Because both scored denominators are zero, hinted minus placebo is undefined;
+there is no evidential conclusion about the decomposition hint yet. The structural
+hint only names the nearly constant 60-cell histogram and common paired-block
+incidence pattern; it does not give a procedure.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+import gen_2601_04295 as g
+
+params = g.DIFFICULTY[g.SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY]
+inst = g.make_instance(seed=123, **params)
+question = g.render(inst)
+answer = g.parse_answer('<answer>{"pair":[1,2],"block":[1,2,3,4,5,6]}</answer>')
+ok, reason = g.verify(inst, answer)
+```
+
+After a successful hardening rerun, emit from the repository root with:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 2601.04295 20
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+The family is easy with ordinary code: scanning is deliberately the disclosed
+reference algorithm. It may also become easy without tools if a solver recognizes
+the consecutive 60-point construction or can reliably search thousands of prompt
+tokens attentionally. The `P(guess)` figure is only for the declared prior—uniform
+selected pairs times uniform listed blocks—and does not model theorem-aware guesses.
+
+The decoy augmentation and disjoint composition are certificate-preserving
+transformations, not hardness results in the paper. No SAT/ILP/spectral attack is
+called for by this incidence-search objective; the exact domain-standard scan was
+run instead. The canonical key uses six rounds of bipartite color refinement,
+which is invariant under tested relabellings but is not a complete hypergraph
+isomorphism algorithm and could theoretically over-collapse nonisomorphic inputs.
+Most importantly, vendor hardness and the G9 hint diagnostic remain unmeasured
+until the OpenRouter quota problem is fixed.
