-| Intended intuition | decomposition into 12-point/four-triple components |
-| Domain essentiality | native |
-| Reduction | none |
+This directory contains a preserved, locally verified prototype for Gomes,
+[“An explicit family of 30 blocks meeting every 6-set of [60] in at least two
+points”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.04295), but it is **not a shippable problem
+family**. Read [REJECTED.md](REJECTED.md) for the full quantitative decision.
 
-## Problem and provenance
-
-[Gomes, “An explicit family of 30 blocks meeting every 6-set of [60] in at
-least two points”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.04295) partitions ten six-point
-base blocks into five pairs. Section 2 splits each base block into two triples
-and adds the four cross-base triple unions. Equivalently, each paired couple is
-a 12-point component split into four triples, and its two base blocks plus four
-recombinations are exactly all six unions of two triples. Theorem 1 proves that
-any six selected points contain a pair in a listed block.
+The paper's Section 2 object is native and retained: each 12-point component is
+split into four triples and carries all six unions of two triples. The prototype
+composes these components, inverse-plants a unique pair-and-block witness, and
+carries it through an exact modular relabeling. Every local correctness,
+guess-resistance, adversary, scaling, canonicalization, and output-cap gate
+passes.
 
-An instance composes those exact 12-point components and shuffles all blocks.
-Exactly one component gets two selected points in distinct triples; every other
-component gets at most one. The answer is the two selected points and their
-unique pair-of-triples block. Checking types, ordering, selected membership,
-listed-block membership, and containment is exact and cheap. Generation samples
-the answer-bearing component first and carries its union block; it never solves
-the emitted instance. Every distractor block comes from the same Section 2
-component construction as the witness block.
+The family nevertheless fails H. Track A is unavailable because Theorem 1's
+proof is itself a short witness algorithm and the paper proves no hard regime.
+For Track B, the reference incidence scan is `O(6m)` and used a measured mean of
+5,849 tests at `easy` and 17,295 at `hard`; the modular/decomposition shortcut
+uses at most 295 exact operations. The mandatory no-tool pool found that
+shortcut and solved 9/9 calls—3/3 at each of `easy`, `medium`, and `hard`.
+`.meta.json` records the resulting `too_easy` verdict.
 
-## Why Track B, not Track A
-
-The paper contains no hardness theorem or hard parameter regime. Its Section 3
-proof is itself a short witness algorithm on the fixed 60-point example, so a
-Track A claim would be false. This module instead grows a certificate-safe
-haystack by disjoint composition.
-
-The disclosed reference algorithm scans the shuffled blocks and tests their six
-entries against the selected set. It is exact, succeeds 8/8 as expected, and is
-`O(6m)` for `m` blocks. At the shipping preset it averaged **3,688 membership
-tests** and **0.0002–0.0011 seconds** across repeated runs. After recognizing Section 2's component
-decomposition, a no-tool solver scans the sorted selected values for the one
-repeated 12-point quotient and writes the union of the two corresponding
-triples. The measured maximum was 198 exact operations; the conservative bound
-is **238**. That mechanical-versus-compact gap is the Track B claim.
-
-The easy regime that had to be avoided is the paper's single five-component
-instance: its complete block scan is only 180 memberships, and its proof route
-is shorter still. Padding adds only exact paper components, so it lengthens the
-scan without lengthening the eight-atom certificate or the compact route.
-
